course: biology
lesson: 127
title: Madagascar: real lemurs, the aye-aye and the fossa
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In the film Madagascar, King Julien dances at a lemur party, tiny Mort clings to his feet, and the hungry foosa chase them all; every one of these animals is real.
explain: Madagascar broke away from other land millions of years ago, and the animals that reached it, maybe on floating rafts of plants, slowly changed into new kinds found nowhere else.
explain: With no monkeys or woodpeckers on the island, lemurs spread into many kinds: the aye-aye taps on bark with a long thin finger to find grubs, and sifakas bound sideways across the ground like dancers.
explain: Real ring-tailed lemurs are led by females, not kings, sunbathe each morning with arms spread wide, and male rivals hold «stink fights» by waving smelly tails.
explain: The sad true part is that most kinds of lemurs are endangered, mainly because their forests are being cut down.
fact: Ring-tailed lemurs live in groups of about 6 to 30 led by females, who get first choice of food.
fact: Wild lemurs live only on Madagascar and a few small islands nearby, and about 90 percent of Madagascar's wild animals and plants live nowhere else.
fact: In the morning ring-tailed lemurs sit with arms open and bellies to the sun to warm up, because their belly fur is thin.
fact: Male ring-tailed lemurs have «stink fights», rubbing their tails on smelly wrist glands and waving them at rivals; the tail is longer than the body and cannot grab; it is used for balance and signalling.
fact: Mouse lemurs are among the smallest primates; the smallest weighs about 30 grams, and big eyes help them hunt at night; some sleep through the dry season on fat stored in their tails.
fact: The aye-aye taps on bark, listens for grub tunnels, gnaws a hole and pulls the grub out with its long thin middle finger, doing the job of a woodpecker.
fact: The aye-aye's teeth keep growing like a rodent's, so scientists first thought it was a kind of squirrel.
fact: Sifakas leap about 10 metres between trees and on the ground bound sideways on two legs, because their legs are much longer than their arms.
fact: Lemurs are primates but not monkeys; they belong to an older branch of the primate family, and most have a wet nose and a toothcomb.
fact: The fossa is real: Madagascar's biggest predator, a slim cat-like animal with a long tail, related to mongooses, which climbs head-first down trees and hunts lemurs.
fact: Most kinds of lemurs are endangered, mainly because their forests are being cut down, and they are among the most threatened mammals in the world.
fact: Some lemurs drink nectar and carry pollen on their faces from flower to flower, pollinating plants as bees do.
exercise: Who leads a real group of ring-tailed lemurs? | The females | A king like Julien | The oldest male | The youngest baby
exercise: How big is a group of ring-tailed lemurs? | About 6 to 30 | About 2, a pair | About 500 or more | Always exactly 12
exercise: Why do ring-tailed lemurs sit with arms spread to the morning sun? | To warm up through their thin belly fur | To pray quietly to the rising sun each morning | To dry their wet fur after a night of heavy rain | To show off their white bellies to the fossa
exercise: What is a lemur «stink fight»? | Males wave tails rubbed on smelly wrist glands at rivals | Lemurs throw handfuls of mud at each other until one gives up | Lemurs roll in rotten fruit | Females spray rivals with water
exercise: Can a ring-tailed lemur hang from a branch by its tail? | No, its tail is for balance and signalling, not grabbing | Yes, like many monkeys, it hangs by its tail to reach fruit | Yes, but only babies | Yes, it sleeps hanging by its tail
exercise: About how much does the smallest mouse lemur weigh? | About 30 grams | About 3 kilograms | About 300 grams | Only about 3 grams
exercise: Why does the aye-aye tap on tree bark? | To listen for hollow tunnels made by grubs | To call other aye-ayes from far across the forest | To scare away birds | To sharpen its finger
exercise: Which job does the aye-aye do on Madagascar? | The job of a woodpecker, finding grubs in wood | The job of a bee, carrying pollen from flower to flower | The job of a squirrel, burying nuts | The job of an owl, hunting mice
exercise: Why did scientists first think the aye-aye was a squirrel? | Its teeth keep growing like a rodent's | It buries nuts in the ground for the winter | It has a bushy red tail | It lives in tree holes
exercise: Why do sifakas hop sideways across open ground? | Their legs are much longer than their arms | They are dancing to music, just like King Julien | Their feet are too hot | They are trying to look tall
exercise: Are lemurs monkeys? | No, they are primates from an older branch | Yes, they are small monkeys that live in Africa | No, they are a kind of cat | No, they are a kind of rodent
exercise: What is the fossa? | Madagascar's biggest predator, related to mongooses | A kind of small lion that lives in the forest | A dog that came on ships | A kind of large lemur that eats meat instead of fruit
exercise: Why are Madagascar's animals found nowhere else? | The island broke away long ago and its animals changed alone | People brought them all from Africa in boats a few years ago | They were all made in a zoo | They hid from other countries
exercise: How might the first animals have reached Madagascar? | Floating across the sea on rafts of plants | Walking over a land bridge last year | Flying in on aeroplanes | Swimming from India in one day
exercise: Why are most kinds of lemurs endangered? | Their forests are being cut down | They are all eaten by hungry lions | They forget how to climb the trees | The island has grown too cold for them
exercise: How can a lemur pollinate a flower? | It carries pollen on its face from flower to flower | It plants the seeds with its hands in neat little rows | It eats the flower whole | It shakes the tree until pollen falls
test: «Julien is king so real lemur groups have a king, obvs» What is right? | Real ring-tailed lemur groups are led by females | Right, the loudest male is the king of the whole group | Right, there is one king per island | Right, kings are chosen by dancing
test: «6 ring-tailed lemurs + 2 fossas, how many tails» How many? | 8 | 6 | 2 | 16
test: «aye-aye's got how many long thin middle fingers then, it has 2 hands» How many? | 2 | 1 | 10 | 4
test: «lemurs = monkeys from Africa innit» What is right? | Lemurs are primates but not monkeys, and live wild only on Madagascar | Right, they are African monkeys that also live in India | Right, they came from the zoo | Wrong, they are cats
test: «sifaka jumped further than a bus, no way» What is right? | Sifakas can leap about 10 metres between trees | Impossible, lemurs cannot jump, they only walk on the ground | They can only hop a few centimetres | Sifakas fly using their tails
test: What would happen to some Madagascar plants if their nectar-drinking lemurs disappeared? | Fewer of their flowers would be pollinated | They would grow much faster and bigger without lemurs | Nothing at all would change | They would grow fur
test: «why can't they just move the lemurs to Africa if the forest is gone» What is the best answer? | Lemurs live wild only on Madagascar, and many need their own small patch of forest | Africa has lots of wild lemurs already, so a few more would be fine | Lemurs prefer cities | Lemurs can live anywhere without trees
test: «the foosa's a made-up monster from the film lol» What is right? | The fossa is a real predator that climbs trees and hunts lemurs | Right, it is a made-up monster, like dragons in other films | It is a real kind of dog | It is a real kind of bear
test: Why might there be lemurs doing a woodpecker's job, but no woodpeckers? | No woodpeckers reached the island, so the aye-aye took on that way of life | Woodpeckers were all eaten by hungry lemurs long ago, so none are left | Woodpeckers hate islands | Lemurs scared all woodpeckers away
test: A tiny mouse lemur is busy at night but sleeps through the dry season. Where does its energy come from? | Fat stored in its tail | Food brought by other lemurs | Sunlight soaking into its fur | Water it drinks in its sleep
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the leadership of groups of ring-tailed lemurs. | Such groups are led by females, which have priority of access to food | Such groups are governed by a single male designated king | Such groups possess no form of leadership whatsoever | Such groups are led by the youngest individual
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the greater part of the fauna of Madagascar occurs nowhere else upon Earth. | The island separated from other land millions of years ago, and its fauna developed in isolation | The fauna was introduced from Africa by human settlers | The fauna was bred in captivity in zoos and subsequently released upon the island | The fauna has deliberately avoided all other territories
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the method of feeding employed by the aye-aye? | It taps upon bark, detects the tunnels of grubs, and extracts them with an elongated middle finger | It extracts nectar from flowers by means of an elongated tongue | It captures fish in shallow streams by stirring the mud with its elongated fingers | It consumes the leaves of grasses at ground level
exam: Ascertain the principal cause of the endangerment of the majority of species of lemur. | The clearance of the forests upon which they depend | Predation by lions introduced from Africa | A failure of the species to learn to climb | A decline in the temperature of the island
